    {
      "skill_id": "pt-b2-028",
      "title": "Combined pronouns and the forms -lo, -la, -no, -na",
      "explanation": "Two object pronouns can fuse. Me plus o gives mo, me plus a gives ma, me plus os gives mos, me plus as gives mas; te gives to, ta, tos, tas; lhe gives lho, lha, lhos, lhas; nos plus o gives no-lo; vos plus o gives vo-lo. The first pronoun is the indirect object and the second the direct object: ele não mo deu, mostrei-lhas. The direct object o, a, os and as also changes after the verb: after r, s or z the ending drops and the pronoun becomes -lo, -la, -los, -las (fazê-lo, lê-lo); after a nasal sound it becomes -no, -na, -nos, -nas (compraram-na). Do not leave the verb ending in front of -lo (fazer-o is wrong).",
      "examples": [
        {
          "pt": "Ele não mo deu.",
          "en": "He did not give it to me."
        },
        {
          "pt": "Mostrei-lhas.",
          "en": "I showed them to him."
        },
        {
          "pt": "Fazê-lo é difícil.",
          "en": "Doing it is hard."
        }
      ],
